<!-- cell: title-page id="38d86852-bfc2-4773-ab59-1c6579ada2dc" title="Plot 14" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="30208ed3-0fb9-4bb0-ac68-9cce3b18a00f" -->

Jo Harland took on Plot 14 at the Ashley Vale allotments the week after she cried in the stationery cupboard at work. She had gone in for a stapler, and the stapler had been the last straw in a quarter of fourteen-hour days, three product launches and a review that called her a natural multitasker.

Plot 14 was a rectangle of nettles and broken cloches between a rhubarb patch and a shed with a sign that said OKAFOR — KNOCK. Jo cleared it in two furious weekends, bought every seed packet in the garden centre and planted the lot in one afternoon, in rows as straight as a spreadsheet.

Everything came up at once, crowded and pale, and then the slugs came, and a week of rain, and by June her tomato seedlings lay flat on the soil like tiny drowned soldiers.

Mr. Okafor knocked on her shed, which she felt was against the spirit of his sign. He was a retired bus driver of seventy-four in a straw hat, and he had grown runner beans on the next plot since before she was born. "You plant like you're late for something," he said. "I am late for something. I'm always late for something."

He handed her a single tomato seedling in a yoghurt pot. "One," he said. "Water it in the evening. Don't touch it in between. Come and tell me in August."

She did as she was told, mostly to prove him wrong. On Sunday mornings she sat on an upturned bucket with a flask of coffee and watched one plant do almost nothing, very slowly, while the allotments hummed with bees and other people's radios. Her phone stayed in the car. Some Sundays she forgot it was there.

In August the plant was taller than she was and hung with fruit so red it looked painted. She ate the first tomato standing up, warm from the sun, and it tasted of nothing she had ever bought.

The next morning she asked her boss for a four-day week. He said yes so quickly that she wondered why she had never asked.

She brought Mr. Okafor a bowl of tomatoes. He bit into one and nodded. "August," he said, as if it had been her idea all along.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="9fd05db7-6ba0-41ff-92a6-6c03c91642f9" title="Jo Harland" words="0-21, 79-107, 108-140, 193-202, 233-243, 290-327, 328-351" -->

A marketing manager worn to the bone who measures herself by speed. She takes on an allotment to keep from breaking, wants everything at once, and learns to want less.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="4643188b-0ae0-4295-ac8f-0c94b29f1672" title="The Ashley Vale allotments" words="0-21, 55-78, 108-140, 141-183, 244-276" -->

Rows of small plots, sheds and rhubarb where things grow at their own pace: Jo's Plot 14 lies between a rhubarb patch and Mr. Okafor's shed.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="2202ed48-c4b2-4071-a084-9095a59cdc14" title="Burnout" words="0-54, 79-107, 193-202, 277-289, 328-351" -->

Jo's exhaustion — fourteen-hour days, constant lateness, crying in a cupboard — which she brings to the garden and leaves there.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="4218c234-174a-47c3-90df-4e1771da5ef2" title="Patience" words="141-192, 203-232, 244-289, 290-327, 352-377" -->

The slow work of letting one plant grow, taught by Mr. Okafor, a retired bus driver who has grown beans for decades: waiting, watching, not touching.

<!-- cell: plot id="ac51f566-2978-46f3-9275-4b5f3ec5638b" title="Plot 14" words="0-21, 79-107, 108-140, 141-192, 203-232, 244-276, 290-327, 328-351" -->

Jo plants everything at once and loses it all; Mr. Okafor gives her a single seedling and tells her to wait; by August it fruits, and she asks for a four-day week.
